Consider relativistic nuclear collisions in which one wishes to predict flavor observables (e.g., yields, spectra, and correlations of strange and other flavored hadrons). Two broad classes of theoretical descriptions are available:

(i) Microscopic reaction models, which follow individual particle (parton or hadron) degrees of freedom through elementary scattering, production, and decay processes specified by cross sections and transition amplitudes.

(ii) Statistical/thermal and kinetic (transport) models, which describe the system through phase-space distribution functions evolving via Boltzmann-type equations, or through equilibrium ensembles characterized by temperature and chemical potentials.

Develop a unified theoretical framework that consistently couples a microscopic description of elementary reaction mechanisms with a statistical or kinetic description of the bulk evolution, in such a way that the framework simultaneously:

(a) accounts for the full collision dynamics, including initial conditions, finite reaction volume, and finite reaction time;
(b) describes the transition from quantum-mechanical evolution at early times and short distances to effectively classical transport at later times and larger scales;
(c) incorporates non-equilibrium effects, including off-equilibrium distribution functions, dissipative corrections, and chemical non-equilibration of flavor degrees of freedom;
(d) is extensible to accommodate previously unidentified reaction mechanisms or new collective phenomena.

Provide such a framework, or prove obstructions to its existence within a precisely specified class of models.

